First expert: Sam Kerr
  1. Bit of an introduction: Who are you/ what do you do?
    My name is Sam; I'm a cyber security professional. I graduated studying Cyber Security in 2022.
    From there I got a job as a Help Desk Technician/consultant at a Perth MSP provider, then I moved into the Cyber Security industry in 2024 working for a big 
    multinational Cyber Security company as a Security Analysist.
    In 2025 I joined Qbit as their first Cyber Security technician, to aid them getting their Cyber Security offering off the ground. 
  2. What projects are you currently working on/ have just finished?
    Current projects involve getting clients SMB1001 certified. SMB1001 is a relatively new global Cyber Security standard that has multiple tiers of assertation.
    More projects recently are with Application whitelisting and vulnerability management for our customers. Especially vulnerability management as that ties in very 
    early in the SMB1001 certification tiers.
  3. What is your thought process/ what are the stages for you when implementing a project?
    When a project comes in, Depending on the job I would be involved in the middle and at the end. For the middle I'll be involved with the configuration of security 
    controls like conditional access policies once their Entra tenant has been set up.
    Where I come in most is at the end where Vulnerability management and Application whitelisting come in. I does depend on the project of course, as if the project 
    is solely a Security uplift I would be doing a majority of the work.
  4. Finally, what difficulties (if any) did you encounter?
    There is not many difficulties in my role, but more considerations with implementing Cyber. Security is great but it can also be overbearing for end users, its 
    about finding the right balance between security and usability.
    More so with Application Whitelisting which is very invasive in its nature. It takes a lot of work to get an environment ready for whitelisting to be enforced. 
    If the right application or process is not approved/allowed properly and you enforce the rule, it could cripple an environment. This is easy to reverse, but its 
    the consideration and testing that has to take place beforehand which can be difficult.

Second expert: Simon Macneall
  1. Bit of an introduction: Who are you/ what do you do?
    Simon Macneall, CTO of Colleagues Matrixx. I oversee the development/technology team at the company.
  2. What projects are you currently working on/ have just finished?
    I've just finished mitigation of issues found during a regular penetration test against one of our core products. 
    These involved fixing:
      a. Access control (System allowing lower level users access to incorrect functionality through query modification)
      b. Blocking Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) issues
      c. Adding security verification (requiring reentry of password) for sensitive actions
  3. What is your thought process/ what are the stages for you when implementing a project?
    The steps taken for this project
      a. Break down the penetration test document into items that need rectifying. 
      b. Prioritising the items based on the severity of the issue (Chance of being seen in the wild and damage caused if implemented by a rogue actor).
      c. Reproduce the issue in our development environment
      d. Rectify the problem
      e. Rerun the penetration test
  4. Finally, what difficulties (if any) did you encounter?
    Some items were hard to reproduce (involving manipulating the DOM in the web browser and updating request parameters to trigger the issue)
